          "type": "output_text",
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"gissing-the-odd-women-a6e5669ceb211458\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":0,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.96,\"analysis\":\"Everard’s “I believe I still love you” is principally a hedged present-state avowal: he presents his continuing love as true while marking some uncertainty about it. The utterance does not itself undertake a commitment, despite occurring immediately before a marriage proposal, and its controlled argumentative delivery provides no independent expressive/reflexive force.\",\"ambiguity\":\"The epistemic hedge “I believe” weakens Everard’s certainty and may suggest imperfect self-knowledge, but it does not alter the utterance’s predominantly truth-conditional mode.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"direct\",\"description\":\"Everard directly addresses Rhoda, saying “I believe I still love you”; the target love proposition is embedded under the epistemic phrase “I believe.”\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"The avowal answers Rhoda’s challenge to Everard’s seriousness during a tense reconsideration of their former relationship. Its proximity to repeated marriage proposals gives it courtship and persuasive significance, but the proposal—not the love avowal itself—carries the clear undertaking force. Rhoda subsequently disputes the sincerity and completeness of his love, and Everard soon resolves to marry Agnes Brissenden, creating contextual doubt about the depth or durability of the state he avows without changing the utterance’s truth-conditional force.\",\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“I loved you, that’s all. And I believe I still love you.”\",\"supports\":\"The present love clause, qualified by “I believe,” explicitly avows a continuing emotional state and strongly supports T.\",\"confidence\":1.0},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The avowal follows Rhoda’s question about whether Everard was serious and is delivered while he answers her steady look.\",\"supports\":\"The utterance is a considered answer within an argument about his feelings, not a reflex-like verbal discharge; this supports E=0.\",\"confidence\":0.97},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Immediately afterward Everard separately asks, “Will you marry me?” and repeats the request.\",\"supports\":\"The text distinguishes the love avowal from the explicit relational undertaking, so the surrounding proposal does not by itself establish P for the core utterance.\",\"confidence\":0.98},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Rhoda later says, “You never loved me with entire sincerity,” and Everard later announces that he will marry Agnes Brissenden.\",\"supports\":\"The narrative context complicates the sincerity and durability of the avowed state, but such possible self-deception or instability is orthogonal to its T-mode.\",\"confidence\":0.94},{\"evidence_id\":\"e5\",\"source\":\"supplied_metadata\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The occurrence is identified as an affirmative present-tense explicit love expression in Chapter XXX of George Gissing’s The Odd Women.\",\"supports\":\"Confirms the target’s present-state grammatical framing and bibliographic location.\",\"confidence\":1.0}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T naturally captures the hedged avowal. Its persuasive relationship context and disputed sincerity require contextual explanation but reveal no missing dimension in the core ontology.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":null}"
        }
